{# App page chrome (S8, 2026-09-04).

   Replaces the .app-page__header masthead on every /app/* route. Jon's
   decision, 2026-09-04: no visible page name. The sidebar already says
   which page you are on, so the heading goes and only the controls
   stay -- the budget page's shape, which is the one screen in the app
   that reads as an app.

   The <h1> is still emitted, visually hidden. Two things need it:
   screen readers, which otherwise land on a page with no title, and
   print.css, which puts it back on paper where there is no sidebar to
   say what the sheet is.

   Usage, inside an element that has an Alpine scope:

     {% from "partials/app-toolbar.njk" import appToolbar %}
     {% call appToolbar("Trash") %}
       <div class="app-toolbar__group">
         <button class="btn btn--ghost">Refresh</button>
       </div>
       <div class="app-toolbar__actions">
         <button class="btn btn--danger">Empty trash</button>
       </div>
     {% endcall %}

   `flush` -- pass true when the page body is one continuous surface
   (a full-width table) so the strip sits directly on it with no gap.

   A page with no controls at all calls it with an empty body: the
   heading is emitted for screen readers and the empty strip removes
   itself (.app-toolbar:empty). #}
{% macro appToolbar(title, flush=false) %}
<h1 class="visually-hidden app-page__h1">{{ title }}</h1>
<div class="app-toolbar{% if flush %} app-toolbar--flush{% endif %}">{{ caller() }}</div>
{% endmacro %}

{# Same strip, but the page's subject is data rather than a label --
   the register's account name, the category page's category, the
   calendar's month. Those name the thing you are looking at, not the
   screen you are on, so they stay visible and small.

   `title` is the screen-reader heading; `subject` is the visible text
   (pass raw HTML, e.g. an x-text span, so it can be Alpine-bound). #}
{# The heading carries the SUBJECT, not just the screen name. A
   verifier found /app/category/ printing a sheet whose heading said
   "Category" and whose visible name was in the toolbar, which print
   hides -- so the printout named neither the screen nor the thing. A
   screen reader had the same problem. #}
{% macro appToolbarSubject(title, subject, flush=false) %}
<h1 class="visually-hidden app-page__h1">{{ title }}: {{ subject | safe }}</h1>
<div class="app-toolbar{% if flush %} app-toolbar--flush{% endif %}">
  <div class="app-toolbar__group">
    <p class="app-toolbar__subject">{{ subject | safe }}</p>
  </div>
  {{ caller() }}
</div>
{% endmacro %}
